<p>This research addresses these gaps by designing an integrated platform where AI and sustainability are not mere add-ons, but foundational architecture components.</p>
<hr>
<h2>3. Research Methodology</h2>
<p>This study adopts the Design Science Research (DSR) paradigm, which is highly suitable for engineering and information systems research aimed at creating innovative artifacts to solve identified organizational problems. DSR emphasizes the creation of a purposeful IT artifact (in this case, the AI-driven smart construction management platform) and its subsequent evaluation.</p>
<h3>3.1 Research Approach and System Development Lifecycle</h3>
<p>The research employs a mixed-methods approach integrated within an Agile system development lifecycle. The DSR process is structured into five iterative phases:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Awareness of Problem:</strong> Systematic literature review and problem definition regarding the lack of integrated AI and sustainability tools in construction management.</li>
<li><strong>Suggestion:</strong> Conceptualization of the system architecture, defining user roles, and identifying required AI and mathematical models for carbon tracking.</li>
<li><strong>Development:</strong> Design of the database schema, UI/UX mockups, and the mathematical/computational logic for the AI modules (scheduling, risk prediction, carbon estimation). <em>Note: This paper focuses on the architectural design and module specification rather than the full commercial coding phase.</em></li>
<li><strong>Evaluation:</strong> Establishing a validation strategy utilizing expert reviews, heuristic evaluation, and scenario-based simulation.</li>
<li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Documenting the system design, discussing implications, and outlining future empirical testing.</li>
</ol>
<h3>3.2 Data Architecture and AI Component Strategy</h3>
<p>To support the AI functionalities, the platform requires a robust data architecture. The methodology involves designing a centralized, cloud-based data lake capable of ingesting:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Structured Data:</strong> Project schedules, budgets, material quantities, and carbon emission factors (e.g., from databases like ICE or Ecoinvent).</li>
<li><strong>Unstructured Data:</strong> Site images, PDFs of contracts, daily logs, and RFIs (Requests for Information).</li>
</ul>
<p>The AI strategy involves utilizing a hybrid approach:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Supervised Learning (Regression/Classification):</strong> For predicting project delays and cost overruns based on historical features (e.g., weather, contractor performance, supply chain status).</li>
<li><strong>Natural Language Processing (NLP/LLMs):</strong> For automated document analysis, RFI routing, and extracting insights from unstructured text logs.</li>
<li><strong>Optimization Algorithms (e.g., Genetic Algorithms):</strong> For schedule optimization balancing time, cost, and carbon output.</li>
</ul>
<h3>3.3 Validation Strategy and Evaluation Metrics</h3>
<p>Since this paper presents the design and conceptual development of the platform, the methodology includes a structured framework for validation. The proposed validation consists of:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Expert Panel Review:</strong> Engaging 15-20 industry experts (project managers, sustainability consultants, AI engineers) to evaluate the system architecture, UI mockups, and module logic using a structured questionnaire based on the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM).</li>
<li><strong>Scenario-Based Simulation:</strong> Feeding historical data from a completed construction project into the platform’s AI algorithms to assess accuracy in predicting the (already known) delays and carbon footprint, comparing the AI’s output against the actual historical baseline.</li>
</ul>
<p>Metrics for evaluation will include Predictive Accuracy (RMSE, MAE for cost/time models), Usability (System Usability Scale - SUS), and Perceived Usefulness for Green Business alignment.</p>
<hr>
<h2>4. System Design and Development</h2>
<p>The proposed AI-Driven Smart Construction Management Platform, developed under the name <strong>NOVA Project Manager</strong>, is designed as a cloud-native SaaS (Software as a Service) solution, comprising a comprehensive web dashboard for office-based project managers and executives, and a mobile application for on-site execution, data collection, and real-time updates. The live deployed version of the NOVA platform can be accessed at: https://sulaxshan45.github.io/NOVA-PM1/</p>
